# `multigrid::Hierarchy::apply`

[Back to `multigrid::Hierarchy`](../Hierarchy.md)

Applies the operator `A` to a field on the finest grid.

## Signature

```cpp
[[nodiscard]] pluss::Series multigrid::Hierarchy::apply(const pluss::Series &u) const;
```

## What It Does

Computes `(A u)ᵢ = −Σₐ hₐ⁻²[b⁺ₐ,ᵢ(u_{i+eₐ} − uᵢ) − b⁻ₐ,ᵢ(uᵢ − u_{i−eₐ})]` (plus `mass·u` if a mass term is set) with the declared end conditions, for the whole field and every batch lane at once. It is the operator you pass to a Krylov solver. On the CPU, for a field that records no derivative and has the template's shape, it runs a fused native stencil; otherwise it is built from `Series` expressions and is differentiable.

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series &u`
: A field whose trailing dimensions are the axis sizes.

## Returns

`A u`, shaped like `u`.

## Errors

- A field of the wrong shape throws.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Second differences. The field `u = x²` on a grid of unit spacing has second derivative 2, and the operator is the negative of the second difference, so `A u = −2` in the interior; the end cells differ because the Neumann ghost cell repeats the end value.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  const Series u = pluss::make_series({0.0, 1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16.0, 25.0, 36.0, 49.0});
  const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = {{8, 1.0, mg::Edge::Neumann, mg::Edge::Neumann}}}, u);
  out.writeln("A u =", h.apply(u).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

A batch. Two lanes are processed together; each is acted on independently.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  const Series u = pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 4.0, 3.0, 2.0, 1.0}, {2, 4});
  const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = {{4, 1.0, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}}}, u);
  out.writeln("lane 0:", h.apply(u).select(0, 0).items<double>());
  out.writeln("lane 1:", h.apply(u).select(0, 1).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

Symmetry. The operator is symmetric: `⟨A u, v⟩ = ⟨u, A v⟩`, which is what lets conjugate gradients use it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const std::int64_t n = 32;
  const Series u = (pluss::series::arange(0.0, 32.0, 1.0, pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * 0.7).sin();
  const Series v = (pluss::series::arange(0.0, 32.0, 1.0, pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * 0.3).cos();
  const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = {{n, 1.0, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Neumann}}}, u);
  out.writeln("<Au,v> =", (h.apply(u) * v).sum().item<double>(), ", <u,Av> =", (u * h.apply(v)).sum().item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

Differentiable. A field marked for differentiation receives a gradient through the operator, because the expression path records it. For `L = Σ A u` the gradient with respect to `u` is `Aᵀ 1 = A 1`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace mg = pluss::subsp::math::multigrid;
namespace krylov = pluss::subsp::math::krylov;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  Series u = pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0});
  u.tracks_slope_(true);
  const mg::Hierarchy h({.axes = {{4, 1.0, mg::Edge::Dirichlet, mg::Edge::Dirichlet}}}, pluss::series::ones({4}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64));
  h.apply(u).sum().reverse();
  out.writeln("gradient =", u.sensitivity().items<double>(), ", A 1 =", h.apply(pluss::series::ones({4}, pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).items<double>());
}
```

